  "result": "I withdrew the record for NRS_484D_400_5 instead of revising it, because no rewrite of this node can pass the gate while the run's reading stays as it is. The withdrawal is on span `s1`, filed as an `atom` gap, so the law goes to the operator.\n\n**Why it can't pass:** the reading counts no vehicle class as a member of `DEF-NRS-484A-020::authorized_emergency_vehicle`. Every class is left undecided (\"unresolved\"), including `ru.emergency_vehicle`, `ru.fire_apparatus` and `ru.motor_vehicle`. As a result:\n- No road user is ever definitely an authorized emergency vehicle, so the siren rule can never be violated or complied with. That rules out the required `forbid` and `permit` scenarios.\n- Any ordinary car is \"unknown\" rather than \"not a member\", so `idle` can't come out as not applicable either.\n\n**Why I didn't swap in `ru.emergency_vehicle`:** the run has accepted \"authorized emergency vehicle\" as an aggregate. The ontology says an accepted aggregate stays an aggregate, so a record can't replace it with that class.\n\n**What the operator needs to decide:** change how the reading classifies this aggregate. For example, count `ru.emergency_vehicle` and `ru.fire_apparatus` as members, and treat `ru.motor_vehicle` as covered or excluded. The alternative is an observable feature showing a vehicle is authorized. This is the reading's call, not the record's. The rest of the planned record (the lamp exception and the siren duty) has no fault the gate reports, and the `branch_1` scenario already passed.\n\nThe withdrawal is written to `record.jsonl` in the repair directory.",
